  {
    "sha": "2fe7afb7fbf7d2bc945b1311db5afd1925bfa8fe",
    "node_id": "C_kwDOVoU8JdoAKDJmZTdhZmI3ZmJmN2QyYmM5NDViMTMxMWRiNWFmZDE5MjViZmE4ZmU",
    "commit": {
      "author": {
        "name": "Mike Kinney",
        "email": "mike.kinney@gmail.com",
        "date": "2026-11-12T06:55:35Z"
      },
      "committer": {
        "name": "Mike Kinney",
        "email": "mike.kinney@gmail.com",
        "date": "2026-11-12T06:55:35Z"
      },
      "message": "feat(post): work offsets ride the stream as a 1-based index; the post writes the word\n\nPARAM WorkOffset now carries the setup's 1-based offset number. A script\nthat declares workOffsetIndex = true receives the number and states\nworkOffsetMax (6 when unstated); the engine refuses a higher offset by\nsetup name. An unflagged script receives G54..G59 for offsets 1-6 exactly\nas before and is refused any offset above 6. Legacy G54..G59 words in a\nstream read as 1-6; any other value is refused by setup name.\n\nAll 11 shipped scripts declare the flag, their maximum and a\nworkOffsetName(n) and map the index themselves (no slotOf / frameOf word\nparsing left). Tormach mill and lathe take offsets 7-9 as G59.1-G59.3\n(LinuxCNC G-code reference); every other dialect declares 6 until its\nextended words are verified against a public manual. The docs state the\n1-based WorkOffset beside the probe's 0-based SetWorkOffset.\n\nSlice 1 of 3; goldens byte-identical.\n\nRefs #931\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01CUsqDAgjpiZbXSvmXLvdmL",
      "tree": {
